# Material for mitt_body.obj.
#
# The texture is GENERATED, not painted or photographed - see
# scripts/make_body_texture.py and README.md. Each texel is classified from the
# mesh's own geometry (height, surface normal, position), so the paint lines up
# with the body by construction rather than by eye.
#
# CORRECTION (2026-09-04). An earlier version of this file stated that gz-sim
# could not render the texture: that it "loads the mesh with no warning of any
# kind and simply appears untextured". That claim was WRONG, and it is worth
# saying so plainly because it was recorded as a fact about the renderer.
#
# gz-sim Harmonic renders mesh textures here perfectly well. The Depot world -
# the warehouse the vehicle drives around - references
# ../materials/textures/WALLS_Albedo.png and friends from Depot.dae by exactly
# this convention, and draws fully textured every session.
#
# What actually suppressed the texture was our own description: the URDF put
# <material name="mitt_white"/> on the mesh visual, and URDF->SDF conversion
# turns that into an SDF <material> whose diffuse colour overrides the mesh's
# own. The body was being painted white by MITT, not left untextured by gz.
# There was also a stale <gazebo><material>Gazebo/White</material> tag, which
# is Gazebo CLASSIC script syntax and has no meaning in Harmonic at all.
#
# Kd is left at white on purpose: renderers multiply the diffuse colour by the
# texture, so anything darker would dim the map.

newmtl mitt_body
	Ns 24.0000
	Ni 1.5000
	d 1.0000
	illum 2
	Ka 1.0000 1.0000 1.0000
	Kd 1.0000 1.0000 1.0000
	Ks 0.1200 0.1200 0.1200
	Ke 0.0000 0.0000 0.0000
	map_Ka mitt_body_texture.png
	map_Kd mitt_body_texture.png
